TI  - Solitary Endobronchial Papilloma: A Rare Entity.
PG  - S120-S122
LID - 10.29271/jcpsp.2019.12.S120 [doi]
AB  - Solitary papillomas of the lower airways are uncommon clinical entities that 
      present a diagnostic challenge because they are not usually considered among the 
      differential diagnoses. Reliable clinical data about their presentation and 
      treatment remain scarce. We describe a case of an elderly male who presented to 
      us with cough and hemoptysis. He had been a smoker for 50 years. Chest radiograph 
      revealed hyperinflated lungs and slight hilar enlargement on the right side. 
      Bronchoscopy revealed a polypoid mass at the origin of the right upper lobe 
      bronchus. Biopsy showed it to be a papillomatous growth composed of squamous 
      epithelial cells. No malignant cells were detected microscopically. Patient 
      declined surgical removal of this papilloma. He was fine on followup visits 
      during the subsequent months. In patients with hemoptysis, solitary endobronchial 
      papilloma should also be considered in the differential diagnosis.
