TI  - Severe Fever with Thrombocytopenia Syndrome Complicated with Pseudomembranous 
      Aspergillus Tracheobronchitis in a Patient without Apparent Risk Factors for 
      Invasive Aspergillosis.
PG  - 3589-3592
LID - 10.2169/internalmedicine.3257-19 [doi]
AB  - Severe fever with thrombocytopenia syndrome (SFTS) is a tick-borne infectious 
      disease. A 91-year-old woman was admitted to our intensive-care unit with SFTS, 
      and she developed dyspnea with wheezes 5 days after admission. Bronchoscopy 
      showed scattered white mold in her central airway. An airway tissue biopsy and 
      culture of bronchial lavage fluid revealed fungal hyphae in the necrotic tissue, 
      confirmed as Aspergillus fumigatus. She was thus diagnosed with pseudomembranous 
      aspergillus tracheobronchitis. She had no common risk factors for invasive 
      aspergillosis (IA). Patients with SFTS, even those without apparent risk factors 
      for IA, may be at risk of developing IA.
