TI  - Williams-Campbell syndrome complicated with pulmonary hypertension and Type 2 
      respiratory failure: An adult case report.
PG  - 795-799
LID - 10.1111/crj.13090 [doi]
AB  - Williams-Campbell syndrome, is a rare disorder characterized by a deficiency of 
      cartilage in subsegmental bronchi, leading to distal airway collapse and 
      bronchiectasis, which typically affects the fourth- to sixth-order bronchi. This 
      article reported a 31-year-old female patient who was diagnosed with 
      Williams-Campbell syndrome with pulmonary hypertension and Type 2 respiratory 
      failure due to extensive cystic bronchiectasis. CT of the thorax showed the 
      affected bronchi had characteristic ballooning on inspiration and collapse on 
      expiration.
