TI  - Employing bioabsorbable grafts in two-stage laryngotracheal reconstruction of 
      pediatric patient with severe subglottic stenosis and history of airway surgery.
PG  - 58-60
LID - S0165-5876(18)30468-3 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.ijporl.2018.09.013 [doi]
AB  - A 16-month old female was referred to our practice for laryngotracheal 
      reconstruction (LTR) for acquired subglottic stenosis (SGS) diagnosed at 4 weeks 
      of age due to prolonged intubation. She has a history of open thoracic repair of 
      congenital tracheoesophageal fistula that was complicated by a pneumothorax and 
      phrenic nerve paralysis. We performed a variation of an anterior and posterior 
      cricoid split LTR with tracheal stenting in order to avoid risks of pulmonary 
      morbidity from costochondral cartilage harvesting. We report the first LTR to use 
      KLS Martin Resorb-XG bioabsorbable implant (poly-L-lactic acid & poly glycolic 
      acid) as a substitute graft for autologous cartilage in a patient with severe SGS 
      and a history of airway surgeries. The patient had an uncomplicated recovery and 
      had a patent trachea on laryngobronchoscopy at 4, 13, and 22 months after 
      surgery.
