TI  - Congenital complete absence of tracheal rings with trifurcate carina: Case report 
      of a rare clinical and endoscopic presentation.
PG  - 1-6
LID - S0165-5876(18)30213-1 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.ijporl.2018.05.009 [doi]
AB  - We describe the case of a child with isolated absence of cartilaginous tracheal 
      rings and a trifurcate carina. At 6 months of age, the patient presented to our 
      multidisciplinary airway clinic with stridor and recurrent severe respiratory 
      infections requiring hospitalization. Radiographs showed airway narrowing. Exam 
      demonstrated biphasic stridor. Flexible fiberoptic laryngoscopy demonstrated only 
      mild laryngomalacia. Operative bronchoscopy demonstrated severe tracheomalacia 
      with absence of any visible tracheal rings and a trifurcate carina. Subsequent CT 
      imaging corroborated these findings and did not demonstrate any other major 
      abnormality. The patient did not require operative intervention and his 
      subsequent course was uncomplicated.
