VI  - 7
IP  - 9
DP  - 2017 Sep
TI  - Progress and prospects of early detection in lung cancer.
LID - 10.1098/rsob.170070 [doi]
LID - 170070
AB  - Lung cancer is the leading cause of cancer-related death in the world. It is 
      broadly divided into small cell (SCLC, approx. 15% cases) and non-small cell lung 
      cancer (NSCLC, approx. 85% cases). The main histological subtypes of NSCLC are 
      adenocarcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma, with the presence of specific DNA 
      mutations allowing further molecular stratification. If identified at an early 
      stage, surgical resection of NSCLC offers a favourable prognosis, with published 
      case series reporting 5-year survival rates of up to 70% for small, localized 
      tumours (stage I). However, most patients (approx. 75%) have advanced disease at 
      the time of diagnosis (stage III/IV) and despite significant developments in the 
      oncological management of late stage lung cancer over recent years, survival 
      remains poor. In 2014, the UK Office for National Statistics reported that 
      patients diagnosed with distant metastatic disease (stage IV) had a 1-year 
      survival rate of just 15-19% compared with 81-85% for stage I.
