TI  - Primary tracheobronchial tumors in children.
PG  - 150-5
LID - S1055-8586(16)00027-5 [pii]
LID - 10.1053/j.sempedsurg.2016.02.013 [doi]
AB  - Primary tracheobronchial tumors are rare lesions that can be benign or malignant, 
      with different location along the airway tree. Symptoms may include wheezing, 
      chronic pneumonia, asthma, chest pain, recurrent cough, atelectasis, haemoptysis, 
      and weight loss. Due to the heterogeneity of symptoms, diagnosis can be difficult 
      and the airway involvement can lead progressively to a bronchial or tracheal 
      obstruction. Due to the rarity of primary tracheobronchial tumors in children, 
      there are not any oncological guidelines on pre-operative work-up, treatment, and 
      follow-up. Only few reports and multicentric studies are reported. In most cases, 
      surgical resection seems to be the treatment of choice. Brachytherapy, endoscopic 
      treatment, and chemotherapy are rarely described. In this article we present an 
      overview on these rare tumors, including pathological aspects, clinical 
      presentation, imaging assessment, and endoscopic or open surgical treatments. We 
      discuss different surgical approaches, according with tumor location.
