TI  - Stridor: a rare presentation of oesophageal malignancy.
LID - 10.1136/bcr-2015-212408 [doi]
LID - bcr2015212408
AB  - A middle-aged ex-smoker, with a history of curative surgery for oesophageal 
      squamous cell carcinoma 7 years earlier, presented to the casualty department at 
      Mater Dei Hospital with stridor and a 2-week history of progressively worsening 
      dyspnoea. A thoracic CT scan showed the presence of a posterior mediastinal mass 
      involving the upper half of the stomach and posterior wall of the trachea. 
      Histology of an exophytic ulcerating lesion at 25 cm of the oesophagus was that 
      of squamous cell carcinoma. Bronchoscopy performed to ascertain the cause of the 
      stridor showed the trachea to be 70% occluded. The patient showed symptomatic 
      improvement with radiotherapy and intravenous dexamethasone; however, he passed 
      away a few weeks later due to respiratory failure secondary to tracheal 
      occlusion.
