TI  - Is bronchoscopy necessary in the preoperative workup of a solitary pulmonary 
      nodule?
PG  - 36-40
LID - S0022-5223(15)00305-0 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.jtcvs.2015.02.060 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVE: This study evaluated the role of conventional bronchoscopy in the 
      preoperative workup of patients with solitary pulmonary nodules (SPNs). METHODS: 
      Patients with SPNs of unknown origin were enrolled for preoperative bronchoscopy 
      at our institution. Bronchoscopic findings were prospectively collected, and 
      their impact on planned therapy was analyzed. RESULTS: A total of 1026 patients 
      were included. Bronchoscopy identified unsuspected findings in 80 (7.8%) of them, 
      with a total of 826 (80.5%) malignant nodules. Referent values for bronchoscopic 
      detection of malignant SPNs were: accuracy, 24.3% (95% confidence interval [CI]: 
      21.7%-27.0%); sensitivity, 5.9% (95% CI: 4.5%-7.4%); specificity, 100%; and 
      negative predictive value, 20.5% (95% CI: 18.0%-22.9%). Malignant bronchoscopic 
      findings were more common in male patients (odds ratio [OR] = 2.1, 95% CI: 
      1.1-3.9, P = .021) and large nodules (OR = 2.3, 95% CI: 1.6-3.3, P < .001). 
      Surgery was cancelled in 2 (0.2%) patients and modified in 36 (3.5%) patients 
      because of bronchoscopic findings. In all, for 268 (26.1%) SPNs that presented 
      with ground-glass opacity, the bronchoscopy was unrevealing. CONCLUSIONS: In the 
      preoperative evaluation of SPN, bronchoscopy is most likely to reveal malignancy 
      in larger SPNs and in male patients. Bronchoscopy is not indicated in SPNs that 
      present with ground-glass opacity on computed tomography scan.
