DP  - 2019 Mar 26
TI  - [Clinical features of primary tracheobronchial pulmonary amyloidosis].
PG  - 918-922
LID - 10.3760/cma.j.issn.0376-2491.2019.12.008 [doi]
AB  - Objective: To analyze the clinical features of primary tracheobronchial pulmonary 
      amyloidosis (PTBA). Methods: The records of 11 patients with PTBA diagnosed by 
      pathology from January 2002 to June 2018 in Xiangya Hospital of Central South 
      University were retrospectively reviewed, including clinical manifestations, 
      laboratory and imaging examination, bronchoscopic manifestations and therapies. 
      Meanwhile, PTBA was staged based on the severity and extent of lesion under 
      bronchoscopy. Results: The most common clinical symptoms were cough and 
      expectoration followed by shortness of breath and hemoptysis. Chest computed 
      tomography (CT) revealed pulmonary infection (4/11), pulmonary nodules and masses 
      (5/11), interstitial lesions (2/11), tracheal bronchial wall thickening (5/11) or 
      airway stenosis (4/11). Bronchoscopy showed mucosal hypertrophy (8/11), nodular 
      bulge (3/11), and luminal stenosis (6/11). According to the lesion involvement, 1 
      case only involved the lungs, 10 cases involved the trachea and/or bronchus, with 
      (8/10) or without (2/10) lung lesions. According to the bronchoscopic staging, 2 
      cases (2/10) in the stage Ⅰ, lesions were limited; 2 cases (2/10) in the stage Ⅱ, 
      lesions were diffuse; 6 cases (6/10) in the stage Ⅲ, diffuse lesions with 
      stenosis. Conclusions: PTBA is a rare disease of the respiratory system, with 
      unspecific clinical manifestations and diverse pulmonary imaging findings; airway 
      mucosal hypertrophy and nodular bulging can be seen under bronchoscopy. Patients 
      with advanced stage may present with airway stenosis.
