TI  - Mistaken Asymptomatic Carinal Foreign Body in a Child.
PG  - e11-e13
LID - 10.1097/PEC.0000000000001707 [doi]
AB  - Foreign body aspirations are commonly seen in emergency departments (EDs) 
      worldwide, presenting with cough, dyspnea, wheeze, and decreased air entry. Chest 
      radiographs are commonly utilized diagnostic tools to confirm foreign object 
      aspiration. The following is a case report of a child who presented in the ED 
      with a carinal push-pin aspiration and a lack of respiratory symptoms; an 
      extremely rare ED presentation of foreign body aspirations masquerading as a 
      foreign body ingestion. This attests to the importance of conducting a lateral 
      chest radiograph for diagnostic purposes to accurately interpret an esophageal or 
      tracheal foreign body placement.
