TI  - The Different Clinical Aspects of Pediatric Primary Airway Tumors in the Larynx, 
      Trachea, and Bronchi.
PG  - 1304-1311
LID - 10.3346/jkms.2017.32.8.1304 [doi]
AB  - Primary airway tumors are rare in children and no literature reviewed their 
      characteristics each location. We evaluate the clinical characteristics and 
      outcomes of Korean children with primary airway tumors, from the larynx to 
      bronchi. A retrospective chart review of children with primary tumors of the 
      larynx, trachea, and bronchi at Asan Medical Center from January 2000 to July 
      2016 was conducted. Nineteen children were diagnosed with primary airway tumors 
      of the larynx (47.4%), trachea (10.5%), and bronchi (42.1%). Median follow-up 
      duration was 2.8 years and there were recurrences in 21.1%. Laryngeal tumors were 
      associated with a younger median age at onset (2 months) and diagnosis (4 
      months), and most were relatively small (median size = 5.3 mm) and symptomatic. 
      Tracheal and bronchial tumors were found in older children (age at onset and 
      diagnosis > 11 years) and large (> 15.0 mm). Most (75%) patients with bronchial 
      tumors were asymptomatic and all the patients with tracheal tumors were 
      symptomatic. This study suggests that we should consider different the locations 
      in primary airway tumor based on the age at onset and diagnosis, initial symptoms 
      or signs, and size of tumor.
