DP  - 2017
TI  - Systemic Air Embolism Following Diagnostic Bronchoscopy.
PG  - 819-821
LID - 10.2169/internalmedicine.56.7836 [doi]
AB  - A 51-year-old man was admitted to have a nodule evaluated using chest computed 
      tomography (CT). Shortly after curetting and transbronchial biopsies via 
      bronchoscopy, hypotension, bradycardia, unconsciousness, and left hemiplegia 
      appeared and resolved within one hour. Head CT showed cerebral air embolism. The 
      following day, lower left quadrant pain developed. Pneumatosis intestinalis on 
      abdominal CT and elevation of creatine kinase and troponin T levels indicated air 
      embolism in the mesenteric and coronary arteries. Some reports have documented 
      cerebral air embolism alone after bronchoscopy; however, we should consider 
      systemic air embolism, even when encountering a patient without specific symptoms 
      related to any organ.
