PMID- 26801254
OWN - NLM
STAT- MEDLINE
DCOM- 20161005
LR  - 20241219
IS  - 1471-2466 (Electronic)
IS  - 1471-2466 (Linking)
VI  - 16
DP  - 2016 Jan 22
TI  - Expert opinion of mediastinal lymph node positions from an intrabronchial view.
PG  - 15
LID - 10.1186/s12890-016-0176-6 [doi]
LID - 15
AB  - BACKGROUND: The knowledge of the mediastinal lymph node positions from an 
      intrabronchial view was important for conventional transbronchial needle 
      aspiration (TBNA). The introduction of endobronchial ultrasound guided 
      transbronchial needle aspiration (EBUS-TBNA) changed the focus from the 
      intrabronchial landmarks to the real life ultrasound images. However when all 
      EBUS reachable lymph nodes are evaluated (mapping), the knowledge of the 
      intrabronchial positions is crucial. The objective of this study was to present a 
      new expert opinion map from an intrabronchial perspective validated by an 
      interobserver variation analysis. METHODS: Physicians who had performed more than 
      30 EBUS-TBNA were included. They marked areas for optimal TBNA sampling on 
      standardized pictures from an intrabronchial perspective. Areas marked by more 
      than 3 of the 14 experts who had performed more than 1000 EBUS provided the data 
      for the map. The map was validated among the experts and the agreement was 
      compared to the agreement among less experienced physicians. RESULTS: There was 
      high agreement (>80%) among the experts in lymph node positions 4 L, 7, 10 L, 11R 
      and 11 L. The agreement for 4R and 10R was low (<70%). The agreement among the 
      most experienced physicians was significantly higher than the less experienced 
      physicians in station 10 L (92% vs. 50%, p:0.01). CONCLUSIONS: It was possible to 
      present a new map of expert opinion for optimal sampling positions in lymph node 
      stations 4 L, 4R, 7, 10 L, 11R and 11 L. All positions except 4R had high 
      agreement. No area was covered by more than 3 of the 14 experts in station 10R.
