TI  - Bronchiolitis obliterans associated with Stevens-Johnson syndrome: a case report.
PG  - 578-81
LID - 10.3349/ymj.2015.56.2.578 [doi]
AB  - We report a case of bronchiolitis obliterans associated with Stevens-Johnson 
      syndrome. A 59-year-old man presented with respiratory distress that gradually 
      worsened over 3 months. He had been diagnosed with Stevens-Johnson syndrome 3 
      months before admission. He had no history of previous airway disease. On 
      physical examination, expiratory breathing sounds were not audible, and a chest 
      X-ray revealed a hyperinflated lung. A pulmonary function test indicated a severe 
      obstructive pattern. Computed tomography scans of inspiratory and expiratory 
      phases of respiration showed oligemia and air trapping, and both were more 
      prominent on expiration view than on inspiration view. The pathogenesis of 
      bronchiolitis obliterans associated with Stevens-Johnson syndrome is largely 
      unknown.
