TI  - Subglottic Stenosis.
PG  - 787-804
LID - S0095-5108(18)31399-X [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.clp.2018.07.013 [doi]
AB  - The subglottis is a narrow region of the pediatric airway that is exquisitely 
      susceptible to the development of airway stenosis. The incidence of acquired 
      subglottic stenosis in the setting of prolonged intubation has significantly 
      decreased because of improved endotracheal tube management protocols. Advances in 
      otolaryngology interventions, such as balloon dilation and endoscopic cricoid 
      split techniques, may allow the avoidance of tracheostomy in patients with mild 
      to moderate subglottic stenosis. However, patients with severe subglottic 
      stenosis are often tracheostomy dependent. Open surgical techniques to treat 
      severe disease, such as laryngotracheal reconstruction and cricotracheal 
      resection, offer high rates of tracheostomy decannulation.
