TI  - Antiangiogenic therapy for primitive neuroectodermal tumor with thalidomide: A 
      case report and review of literature.
PG  - e9272
LID - 10.1097/MD.0000000000009272 [doi]
LID - e9272
AB  - RATIONALE: Peripheral primitive neuroectodermal tumor (PNET) is a kind of small 
      round cell tumor derived from primitive neuroectodermal tumor. PATIENT CONCERNS: 
      PNET is a highly malignant tumor that is subordinated to Ewing sarcoma. It occurs 
      predominantly in soft tissue and bone and rarely in the bronchi and lung. 
      Traditional surgery, radiotherapy, and chemotherapy are used for the treatment of 
      PNET, but are usually ineffective. DIAGNOSES: There was a rare case of a 17 
      year-old man diagnoses with primary pulmonary PNET. INTERVENTIONS: The patient 
      was treated by the remedy treatment with thalidomide after the poor effect of 
      conventional radiotherapy and chemotherapy. OUTCOMES: The patient survived 
      without disease progression for 15 months and was in stable condition. LESSONS: 
      Thalidomide provides a choice for maintenance therapy in PNET.
