DP  - 2018 Mar 15
TI  - Multiple Endotracheal Metastases of Lung Cancer after Bronchoscopic Intervention.
PG  - 845-847
LID - 10.2169/internalmedicine.9542-17 [doi]
AB  - A 65-year-old man presented with obstructive pneumonia due to squamous cell 
      carcinoma of the lung, which invaded the right main bronchus. Argon plasma 
      coagulation was performed, which reduced his symptoms. Right pneumonectomy was 
      performed after preoperative chemotherapy; the pathological diagnosis was T3N1M0 
      Stage IIIA. Fifteen months after surgery, he presented with bloody sputum. 
      Bronchoscopy showed multiple endotracheal tumors, and a pathological examination 
      revealed squamous cell carcinoma; the same histology as the primary lung cancer. 
      We hypothesized that direct implantation during bronchoscopic therapy or 
      hematogenous or lymphatic spread might have led to metastasis. A careful 
      follow-up is required after bronchoscopic therapy.
