TI  - Intrabronchial Valve Treatment for Prolonged Air Leak: Can We Justify the Cost?
PG  - 2867547
LID - 10.1155/2016/2867547 [doi]
LID - 2867547
AB  - Background. Prolonged air leak is defined as an ongoing air leak for more than 5 
      days. Intrabronchial valve (IBV) treatment is approved for the treatment of air 
      leaks. Objective. To analyze our experience with IBV and valuate its 
      cost-effectiveness. Methods. Retrospective analysis of IBV from June 2013 to 
      October 2014. We analyzed direct costs based on hospital and operating room 
      charges. We used average costs in US dollars for the analysis not individual 
      patient data. Results. We treated 13 patients (9 M/4 F), median age of 60 years 
      (38 to 90). Median time from diagnosis to IBV placement was 9.8 days, time from 
      IBV placement to chest tube removal was 3 days, and time from IBV placement to 
      hospital discharge was 4 days. Average room and board costs were $14,605 
      including all levels of care. IBV cost is $2750 per valve. The average number of 
      valves used was 4. Total cost of procedure, valves, and hospital stay until 
      discharge was $13,900. Conclusion. In our limited experience, the use of IBV to 
      treat prolonged air leaks is safe and appears cost-effective. In pure financial 
      terms, the cost seems justified for any air leak predicted to last greater than 8 
      days.
