DP  - 2016 Jan-Mar
TI  - Synchronous existence of granular cell tumor and small cell carcinoma of lung: An 
      unusual entity.
PG  - 90-2
LID - 10.4103/0377-4929.178239 [doi]
AB  - Granular cell tumor (GCT) is a rare benign mesenchymal tumor that uncommonly 
      occurs in the lung and tracheobronchial tree. Small cell carcinoma of lung is a 
      centrally located malignant neoplasm that commonly occurs in elderly smokers. 
      Concomitant existence of both the neoplasm in lung is extremely rare with only 
      one reported case in the literature. Few rare combinations of GCT with other 
      primary bronchogenic carcinomas have also been reported. Clinical symptoms depend 
      upon the site and size of the tumor. Definitive diagnosis is by histopathological 
      and proper immunohistochemical analysis. Identification of this entity is 
      important as treatment requires individual therapy protocols that depend on the 
      presence of metastasis, location of the tumors, and type of bronchogenic 
      carcinoma.
