TI  - Fiberoptic Bronchoscopic Balloon Dilatation of Bronchial Stenosis in Children.
PG  - 260-264
LID - 10.1097/LBR.0000000000000581 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Bronchial stenosis and treatment with balloon dilatation are rarely 
      experienced in children. The aim of this study was to investigate the results of 
      fiberoptic bronchoscopic balloon dilatation (BBD) in children. METHODS: Between 
      January 2016 and March 2018, 7 children diagnosed as having bronchial stenosis 
      and who underwent BBD as the first treatment option were enrolled in the study. 
      RESULTS: A total of 10 BBDs were performed in 7 patients with a median age of 10 
      months. Underlying causes were as follows: prolonged intubation and prematurity 
      in 4 patients, extubation failure in 2 patients, and recurrent pneumonia and 
      malignancy in 1 patient. The only complication was laceration, seen in 2 
      patients. The patients were symptom free for 11 months. CONCLUSION: BBD is safe, 
      effective, and might be the first-line treatment option for children with 
      bronchial stenosis.
