TI  - Massive haemoptysis secondary to mycotic pulmonary artery aneurysm in subacute 
      invasive aspergillosis.
LID - bcr-2017-223088 [pii]
LID - 10.1136/bcr-2017-223088 [doi]
LID - bcr2017223088
AB  - A 68-year-old man, presented with 3 week history of infective symptoms and mild 
      haemoptysis. Past medical history included severe emphysema and a chronic right 
      upper lobe (RUL) cavity. He was discharged from follow-up a year ago in view of 
      clinical and radiological stability; previous bronchoscopic examinations yielded 
      no specific diagnosis. CT scan on admission confirmed complex cavitary 
      consolidation of RUL. He developed massive haemoptysis requiring intubation and 
      ventilation. CT pulmonary angiogram (CTPA) revealed 16 mm RUL pulmonary artery 
      (PA) aneurysm which was successfully embolized. Sputum cultures, aspergillus 
      antigen and rapidity of clinical progression suggested a diagnosis of subacute 
      invasive aspergillosis (SAIA), prompting treatment with Voriconazole. 
      Bronchoscopy showed blood ooze from RUL even after embolization. Unfortunately, 
      patient continued to deteriorate and succumbed to profound septicaemia.
