DP  - 2018 Jun
TI  - Cryptococcus gattii Infection Presenting as an Aggressive Lung Mass.
PG  - 597-602
LID - 10.1007/s11046-017-0233-6 [doi]
AB  - Cryptococcus gattii is an endemic fungus predominantly isolated in the tropical 
      and subtropical regions, causing predominantly pulmonary disease with a 
      predilection for the central nervous system. Herein, we report a case of rapidly 
      progressing C. gattii pneumonia in an immune-deficient but virologically 
      suppressed host with underlying human immunodeficiency viral (HIV) infection, 
      exhibiting various fungal morphologies from bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) 
      cytological specimens. A 51-year-old Chinese male with known HIV disease was 
      admitted to the Singapore General Hospital for evaluation of functional decline, 
      febrile episodes, and a left hilar mass on chest radiograph. Computed tomography 
      (CT) showed consolidation in the apical segment of the left lower lobe. He 
      underwent bronchoscopy and BAL. Positron emission tomography-computed tomography 
      done 10 days after the initial CT showed approximate doubling of the pulmonary 
      lesion. Cytological examination of the fluid revealed yeasts of varying sizes. 
      Subsequent fungal culture from BAL fluid grew C. gattii 10 days later.
