TI  - The use of ultrasound in the management of a life-threatening malignant 
      laryngotracheal obstruction.
PG  - 335-337
LID - 10.1093/icvts/ivx298 [doi]
AB  - Ultrasound is becoming a valuable diagnostic tool in the armamentarium of 
      thoracic surgeons. We report its use to follow stent placement in the 
      laryngotracheal position for management of life-threatening neoplastic 
      obstruction. For this purpose, a 15-MHz linear probe was placed in the midline at 
      the level of the cricoid cartilage. Ultrasound performed daily at bedside showed 
      normal movement of vocal folds and no stent dislocation. This strategy may be an 
      alternative to computed tomography scan or endoscopy to follow stent placement in 
      extrathoracic airways.
