TI  - Medical lung volume reduction for severe emphysema: A review.
PG  - 141-147
LID - S0954-6111(17)30270-6 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.rmed.2017.08.009 [doi]
AB  - Lung hyperinflation is the main pathophysiological mechanism significantly 
      reducing the Quality of Life (QoL)and contributing to increased morbidity and 
      mortality of the emphysematous patients. Therefore, the prime aim of the 
      management of those patients is the deflation of their lungs. There are a number 
      of treatments proven effective to reduce hyperinflation, such as,pharmacotherapy 
      (bronchodilators/deflators), rehabilitation and surgical volume reduction. 
      Recently, methods to reduce lung volume by a bronchoscopic approach had been 
      developed. We review these methods and in particular the endobronchial valves, 
      coils and the sclerosing therapies and provide the current status, as well as, an 
      algorithm of selection of the appropriate patients. We conclude that further 
      large studies are needed in order to verify the effectiveness of these methods 
      and identify the appropriate group of patients that can be benefit by each of 
      these bronchoscopic techniques.
