AB  - Accessory cardiac bronchus (ACB) is a supernumerary bronchus usually arising from 
      right main or intermediate bronchus. We report the case of a 9-year-old male who 
      presented a 6-month history characterized by two right pneumonia episodes 
      followed by persistent productive cough, recurrent bloody sputum, and chest x-ray 
      persistence of a segmental thickening of right inferior lobe. Bronchoscopy 
      revealed no abnormalities. Computed tomography documented an accessory-lobed ACB 
      originating from right lower brochus. Surgical removal of ACB and related 
      parenchyma was approached thoracoscopically and converted to thoracotomy for 
      evidence of a bronchial injury. Two-year follow-up showed no recurrent infections 
      or respiratory symptoms.
